---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Citizen Stewardship"
  description="Two proposals for democratic renewal, offered for public review, criticism, and improvement."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <h1>Citizen Stewardship</h1>
    <p>Two proposals for democratic renewal, offered for public review, criticism, and improvement.</p>
    <p>
      This site is my two cents. These proposals are public drafts. Anyone can read, download, comment,
      disagree, improve, or suggest alternatives. This is not about one party winning. This is about asking
      whether the rules themselves are broken.
    </p>
    <div class="btn-row">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/proposal-one/">Read Proposal One</a>
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/proposal-two/">Read Proposal Two</a>
      <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/community/">Join the Discussion</a>
      <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/submit/">Submit an Idea</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>What this is</h2>
    <p>This is a citizen-created public review hub for two civic reform drafts.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="section grid">
    <article class="card">
      <h3>Why this exists</h3>
      <p>Public frustration is widespread, but serious proposals often stay fragmented. This project puts concrete drafts in one public place for real scrutiny.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="card">
      <h3>Why something has to change</h3>
      <p>If the rules reward opacity, money, and insider leverage, trust keeps collapsing. Structural repair has to be discussable in plain language.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="card">
      <h3>Why neither party gets a free pass</h3>
      <p>Both major parties have failed ordinary Americans by preserving systems that benefit insiders, donors, corporations, party machinery, and career politicians. The goal is not to replace one tribe’s power with another tribe’s power. The goal is to make public power harder to buy, harder to hide, harder to abuse, and easier for ordinary people to understand.</p>
    </article>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>What you can do here</h2>
    <p>Read the proposals, download the PDFs, challenge weak points, suggest stronger alternatives, and help improve the drafts.</p>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
